WEBVTT


00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:06.300
One often overlooked stitch pattern is
thread lace, also called punch lace.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:10.830
This is for automatic patterning
machines, punch card or electronics.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:15.780
The technique is similar to
fair isle, but it has no floats.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.750
It combines a fine yarn with a heavier
yarn, the stitches knit with the fine yarn

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.600
create open spaces that look like holes.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.790
No stitches are transferred.

00:00:27.375 --> 00:00:30.435
And set up correctly with
a good choice of yarns.

00:00:30.675 --> 00:00:33.345
You'll easily knit, a lace-like fabric.

00:00:36.255 --> 00:00:40.095
Again, similar to fair
isle needles are selected.

00:00:40.995 --> 00:00:45.795
Needles that are selected in middle
position are knit with the yarn from the

00:00:45.795 --> 00:00:48.345
front feeder, which is our thin yarn.

00:00:49.605 --> 00:00:52.965
And the yarn and the back
feeder, the thicker yarn doesn't

00:00:52.965 --> 00:00:55.065
knit, those selected needles.

00:00:56.895 --> 00:01:00.915
Unlike fair isle, needles that
are in working position knit,

00:01:01.155 --> 00:01:04.245
both yarns eliminating the floats.

00:01:05.955 --> 00:01:11.565
In this example, I used a finer yarn and
a similar green color in the heavier yarn.

00:01:12.795 --> 00:01:14.265
Something to consider.

00:01:15.105 --> 00:01:20.414
For most of the design, both yarns are
knitting together at the same time.

00:01:21.089 --> 00:01:24.600
Be sure to keep this in mind when
you're selecting the weights of yarn

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:30.270
to combine, this is an interesting
fabric, but we can improve on this.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.729
I replaced the thin green
yarn with a variegated thread.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.880
This is actual quilting thread on cones.

00:01:39.419 --> 00:01:41.729
I think it's designed
for long arm quilting.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.270
This is a much more interesting fabric.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.960
You can clearly see the
individual stitches that are

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.000
knit with the thread alone.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.550
Here's another example.

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:59.880
At this gauge a heavier yarn is
jazzed up with the variegated thread.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.200
That's the bulky machine.

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:05.750
But here's my favorite.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.509
Take a look at thread
lace on the standard.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:11.750
Same concept, but I really love this.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.590
White sewing thread with a white yarn.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.440
White yarn with a colored sewing thread.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.350
White yarn with another
verigated sewing thread.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:34.620
You can really see how knitting the two
yarns together changes the white knitting

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.140
and it makes it something really special.

00:02:39.089 --> 00:02:43.320
Now in the past, this technique
has always been demonstrated, being

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.989
worked with a fine, clear filament.

00:02:46.649 --> 00:02:49.019
Because the filament
didn't have any color.

00:02:49.019 --> 00:02:50.970
It really does look like lace.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.600
But I don't know about you,
it never appealed to me.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.019
I couldn't get excited
about this technique until

00:02:58.019 --> 00:02:59.940
I played with sewing thread.

00:03:01.934 --> 00:03:04.994
Looking at stitch patterns that
are good for this technique.

00:03:05.355 --> 00:03:10.065
You're not going to want too many needles
next to each other being selected.

00:03:10.904 --> 00:03:13.605
Notice that the white
square is the heavier yarn.

00:03:13.725 --> 00:03:16.484
The black squares
represent the finer yarn.

00:03:17.475 --> 00:03:21.344
So you don't want a bunch of
black squares next to each other.

00:03:24.065 --> 00:03:25.115
Here at the machine,

00:03:25.265 --> 00:03:28.145
there are four stitches that
are knit with the finer yarn,

00:03:28.535 --> 00:03:30.785
but there's a long float behind.

00:03:31.385 --> 00:03:35.105
So keep this in mind when selecting
patterns to use for thread lace.

00:03:37.085 --> 00:03:38.495
I hope you'll give this a try.

00:03:38.855 --> 00:03:41.825
Thread lace is an interesting
alternative to other stitch

00:03:41.825 --> 00:03:44.105
patterns and plain old stockinette.

00:03:44.915 --> 00:03:47.885
And I'm sure that you can come
up with some other beautiful

00:03:47.885 --> 00:03:50.105
combinations for your knitting.

